(511632, 48568, 3569, '<td valign="top">Critique Club review:<br/> <br/>Hi Ed,<br/> <br/>First your shot fits the challenge well. The soft focus on the mouse is quite what I expect in a soft focus shot.<br/> <br/>The subject is good.  The softness on the snout and eye maintain nice details. The colors of the mouse are also nice.  Your cropping, dof, and sharpness are good.<br/> <br/>Now the downfalls, IMO the lighting is either too bright, especially on right side of mouse.  This is somewhat distracting.  Also, the shadow from the snout onto the shirt of the mouse is probably a bit too dark. For some reason, my eye is drawn to the dark area on the shirt, not the eye or head of the mouse (assumed subject).  I''m not sure, but a different colored back ground may have changed this or different angle of lighting.<br/> <br/>Overall, I liked the photo and hope this helps a bit on your next try.<br/> <br/>Merry Christmas,<br/> <br/>Van</td>', 'Critique Club review: Hi Ed, First your shot fits the challenge well. The soft focus on the mouse is quite what I expect in a soft focus shot. The subject is good.  The softness on the snout and eye maintain nice details. The colors of the mouse are also nice.  Your cropping, dof, and sharpness are good. Now the downfalls, IMO the lighting is either too bright, especially on right side of mouse.  This is somewhat distracting.  Also, the shadow from the snout onto the shirt of the mouse is probably a bit too dark. For some reason, my eye is drawn to the dark area on the shirt, not the eye or head of the mouse (assumed subject).  I''m not sure, but a different colored back ground may have changed this or different angle of lighting. Overall, I liked the photo and hope this helps a bit on your next try. Merry Christmas, Van', '2003-12-12 16:52:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(511869, 49089, 12253, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club:<br/> <br/>This is a very good shot of this dog and a good example of soft focus.  The technique and tones are very good.  However, I think the image would be improved if there was a little more shadow detail (in the dark fur).  Also, there''s some overexposed areas on the nose, but I don''t find that to be significant here aesthetically. <br/> <br/>General aesthetics are excellent.  The expression and capture is good enough to make us all care for this dog who we don''t know.  That''s perhaps the most significant accomplish here.  A picture that appeals to many.<br/> <br/>Regards--Neil<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club: This is a very good shot of this dog and a good example of soft focus.  The technique and tones are very good.  However, I think the image would be improved if there was a little more shadow detail (in the dark fur).  Also, there''s some overexposed areas on the nose, but I don''t find that to be significant here aesthetically.  General aesthetics are excellent.  The expression and capture is good enough to make us all care for this dog who we don''t know.  That''s perhaps the most significant accomplish here.  A picture that appeals to many. Regards--Neil', '2003-12-13 00:51:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(512049, 48815, 10402, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>The first thing that I notice when viewing this image are the beautiful warm colors. They draw me right in and work, along with the soft focus and predominantly horizontal lines, to give the photo a very peaceful feeling. The exposure and focus are great, and the sharpness (actually the lack thereof) is well controlled. The composition is lacking; putting the horizon right in the middle of the frame makes the photo seem split in half. The subtle clouds are very nice, but I think the overall photo would be more effective if most of the sky was cropped out. This would raise the horizon and make the buildings a more integral part of the photo. I also think it would have been better in a horizontal format (not cropped--the reflections are an essential part of the composition--but more expansive, including more buildings to the right and/or left).</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! The first thing that I notice when viewing this image are the beautiful warm colors. They draw me right in and work, along with the soft focus and predominantly horizontal lines, to give the photo a very peaceful feeling. The exposure and focus are great, and the sharpness (actually the lack thereof) is well controlled. The composition is lacking; putting the horizon right in the middle of the frame makes the photo seem split in half. The subtle clouds are very nice, but I think the overall photo would be more effective if most of the sky was cropped out. This would raise the horizon and make the buildings a more integral part of the photo. I also think it would have been better in a horizontal format (not cropped--the reflections are an essential part of the composition--but more expansive, including more buildings to the right and/or left).', '2003-12-13 10:39:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(512109, 49011, 10402, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Soft focus combine with high key and low contrast to make this image convey a transcendent, tranquil beauty. The background color is perfect for this message. The composition is just right. The leaf is close enough to the bottom of the frame that it doesn''t draw undue attention, but provides support and context for the flowers. The bud going to the right at the top nicely balances the left-facing flowers.<br/> <br/>Overall, a wonderfully sublime image. I like it very much. My only suggestion is to ignore those who would prefer a more conventional photo, which may indeed be beautiful but would lack the ethereal, spiritual effect you have succeeded in capturing here.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Soft focus combine with high key and low contrast to make this image convey a transcendent, tranquil beauty. The background color is perfect for this message. The composition is just right. The leaf is close enough to the bottom of the frame that it doesn''t draw undue attention, but provides support and context for the flowers. The bud going to the right at the top nicely balances the left-facing flowers. Overall, a wonderfully sublime image. I like it very much. My only suggestion is to ignore those who would prefer a more conventional photo, which may indeed be beautiful but would lack the ethereal, spiritual effect you have succeeded in capturing here.', '2003-12-13 13:26:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(512119, 49164, 2634, '<td valign="top">Hey Gordon,<br/>This is my first "critique club" image, so here goes:<br/> <br/>The image has good use of duotone.  Composition is also very good. I really like the shadows on the building. <br/>While I know you intentionally added grain, I''m not sure it is effective here. I notice it mainly in the sky. Also, could be due to the stockings. <br/>I see that you used stockings over the lens, just wish the effect was a little more noticable. <br/>In this challenge I think voters needed an "in your face" soft focus....your''s, though effective for the shot, was not noticable enough for them.<br/>BTW, I am not thrilled to receive your''s as my first, because I know you are already a great photographer, and what ever you did with this shot was your intention. I can''t help or teach you anything you don''t already know. <br/>Keep it up :)<br/>Terry</td>', 'Hey Gordon,This is my first "critique club" image, so here goes: The image has good use of duotone.  Composition is also very good. I really like the shadows on the building. While I know you intentionally added grain, I''m not sure it is effective here. I notice it mainly in the sky. Also, could be due to the stockings. I see that you used stockings over the lens, just wish the effect was a little more noticable. In this challenge I think voters needed an "in your face" soft focus....your''s, though effective for the shot, was not noticable enough for them.BTW, I am not thrilled to receive your''s as my first, because I know you are already a great photographer, and what ever you did with this shot was your intention. I can''t help or teach you anything you don''t already know. Keep it up :)Terry', '2003-12-13 13:50:51.000000', NULL, 0),
